-- title:
--   Subgroup obstruction to Cayley isomorphism
-- statement:
--   Let $G$ be an abelian group. For $a,b,c,h\in G$, assume $2h=0\ne h$, $a\ne b,b+h,c,c+h$, $3(a-c)\in\{0,h\}$, $b\in\langle a,h\rangle$, and $b\ne c,c+h$. There is no bijection preserving all directed Cayley arcs between supports $\{a,b,b+h\}$ and $\{a,c,c+h\}$. $$\operatorname{Cay}(G,\{a,b,b+h\})\not\cong\operatorname{Cay}(G,\{a,c,c+h\}).$$ No finiteness hypothesis is imposed.
-- source:
--   Alexandre Couret, https://github.com/alexcour/couret-finite-verifications/blob/65b828d9ffd016ce2a1b05403f303fa5f948c10f/research/correspondants_20261013/lean/CayleyTwoColourAbelian.lean#L751-L811, lines 751–811; source file SHA-256 c9643d6e3a677d5a903623825d50a309d87175a21ace2b7ab56939d9189f13b1; theorem Couret.CayleyDoublet.abelianNoIso_of_doublet_mem_closure

